[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Martins et al., 2016-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4175498ce07b5bd73a875316c29994baf43382b8)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 7
[method]
The paper used an EST-based transcriptomic target-discovery workflow for Fusarium graminearum. It assembled 57,282 expressed sequence tags from four wheat-Fusarium interactions at early infection into 34,970 unigenes, separated plant and fungal sequences by BLASTx against wheat and Fusarium reference genomes, and retained 5,094 Fusarium contigs. These expressed sequences were combined with 13,321 genome-predicted proteins, 1,369 secretome proteins, and PHI-base pathogenicity, virulence, and effector sequences. Candidate targets were filtered by early in planta expression, BLAST2GO functional and subcellular localization criteria favoring cytoplasmic or chemically accessible proteins, homology to PDB structures, manual curation of gene copies, protein length under 500 amino acids, and removal of orthologs with over 60 percent sequence identity. The retained targets were then used in structure-based virtual screening: 10,240 compounds were docked into binding sites of the selected targets, ranked by docking score, and the top 15 compounds per target were retained. This relates to the proposed transcriptomic analysis because both use fungal expression data to prioritize candidate genes and biological processes, but the paper used infection-induced ESTs rather than quinofumelin-treated RNA-seq, and its virtual screening is analogous to the plan's molecular docking step rather than a direct quinofumelin binding assay.
[results]
The EST filtering reduced 34,970 unigenes to 5,094 Fusarium-related contigs after removing 10,242 wheat-similar contigs and 951 unknown sequences. The integrated target-selection pipeline yielded three representative Fusarium proteins for further analysis: endoglucanase-5, trichodiene synthase, and ERG6-D-(24)-sterol C-methyltransferase. Virtual screening of 10,240 compounds against these targets produced 45 top-scoring ligands, 15 per target, selected as candidates for experimental validation based on docking scores and interactions with polar, aromatic, and hydrophobic residues. No statistical significance values, baseline comparisons, or quinofumelin-specific binding data were reported.
[context]
The EST data came from 18 cDNA libraries constructed from Fusarium graminearum-infected wheat varieties Bobwhite, Piko, Sumai-3, and Gottingen-2 at the early infection stage. The target dataset included the F. graminearum PH-1 genome, in planta ESTs, a refined secretome, and PHI-base sequences, with PHI-base reported as 682 proteins in the dataset summary and 795 sequences in the methods text. Computational filters used BLASTx, BLAST2GO, PDB homology search, manual curation, a maximum protein size of 500 amino acids, and an ortholog elimination threshold of greater than 60 percent sequence identity. Virtual screening used major conformer populations and docking-score ranking. No specific computational hardware, software versions, docking parameters, or statistical thresholds for compound ranking were provided. The study did not include quinofumelin treatment, RNA-seq, DESeq2, KEGG enrichment, metabolomics, gene deletion, SPR, or MST, so its findings provide a related target-prioritization and docking framework rather than direct evidence for quinofumelin's mode of action.
[end related work 2]

