- in_text_citation: [(Genga et al., 2011-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6185c631b6b043ed076b59058825721c24230441)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 35
[method]
The paper describes a metabolomics workflow based on hyphenated LC-MS, especially LC-DAD-MS and HPLC-MS, for broad metabolite profiling, combined with multivariate statistical analysis such as PCA, HCA, and, in the data-treatment discussion, PLS-DA and OPLS-DA for class separation and variable selection. In the most detailed LC-MS example, root extracts from Salvia miltiorrhiza were prepared using four solvent-based extraction methods and analyzed by 1H-NMR and LC-DAD-MS after water depletion and different drying treatments; PCA score plots were used to discriminate samples, and detected metabolites were compared across treatments. This is methodologically related to the proposed widely targeted LC-MS metabolome analysis of quinofumelin-treated Fusarium graminearum, which also uses differential accumulation between treated and control groups and supervised PLS-DA with P<0.05 and fold change at least 1.5 to select candidate metabolites for KEGG pathway enrichment.
[results]
LC-DAD-MS detected 44 secondary metabolites in Salvia roots, including 5 polyphenolic acids, genipin, umbelliferone, and tormentic acid that had not previously been described in this plant. 1H-NMR identified 28 primary metabolites, described as sugars, amino acids, and carboxylic acids, and 4 secondary metabolites, described as polyphenols. PCA score plots from both NMR and LC-DAD-MS separated freeze-dried, air-dried, and sun-dried samples. Air-drying increased proline, alanine, and succinate and decreased n-butanol and lactate; air-drying also increased sucrose and glutamine, while sun-drying increased leucine, melibiose, and raffinose. LC-DAD-MS showed that air-drying enhanced oligomeric caffeic acids and tanshinones, whereas sun-drying promoted tanshinones but inhibited polyphenolic acids.
[context]
The samples were roots of Salvia miltiorrhiza Bunge subjected to water depletion and then air-dried, sun-dried, or freeze-dried, with freeze-drying used as the control; four extraction methods based on different solvents were compared. The study was a plant metabolomic comparison, not a fungicide-treated fungal system, and the reported statistical separation was based on PCA rather than PLS-DA with P<0.05 and fold change at least 1.5; KEGG pathway enrichment was not reported for this specific LC-DAD-MS example. The paper does not specify the exact LC-MS instrument model, chromatographic conditions, or statistical software used for this example. The relevance to the research plan is that it demonstrates LC-MS can detect broad primary and secondary metabolite changes and that multivariate plots can separate treatment groups, supporting the proposed use of LC-MS and supervised multivariate screening to identify quinofumelin-affected pathways and candidate metabolites for recovery experiments and target validation.
[end related work 2]

