  "support": "The strongest support for the proposed Gene Deletion Studies comes from the newest related work, [(Wang et al., 2024-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c825b35e476e9f1a2d424115887c5cfd63259fde), which used construction of gene deletion mutants in F. graminearum to test TRI-cluster positive regulators and UPR/HOG pathway components in toxisome-shaped structure formation and DON biosynthesis, and showed that the chemical ZJU212 blocks toxisome formation and DON biosynthesis. This directly supports the plan's logic of using deletion mutants to validate pathway involvement after chemical treatment of F. graminearum PH-1 with quinofumelin at 1 μg/mL, although it does not provide metabolite-rescue data. The next strongest support is [(Wang et al., 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/69e2e73cb03a1faa61b26d3c046a41bdcf6f7429), which generated an Fg62 deletion mutant and complemented strain in F. graminearum, verified transformants by PCR, and connected Fg62 to virulence, early-infection expression, and signal-peptide-dependent cell death in N. benthamiana and soybean; this supports the proposed use of deletion plus complementation and PCR verification for candidate quinofumelin target genes, but it does not specify selection markers, protoplast protocols, or metabolite supplementation. Older work by [(Li et al., 2019-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/74f9c1b579ff2eac7825af94b8513e54767128cd) supports the same experimental system and phenotypic logic by constructing FgSvf1 deletion, overexpression, and complementation strains in F. graminearum and characterizing conidial germination, sexual reproduction, wheat-head FHB progression, salt/carbon-source growth, PEP, and intracellular ROS, which parallels the proposed mutant phenotyping on various media, but it did not test quinofumelin or metabolite rescue. Finally, [(Lee et al., 2022-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1a680a3d1713d5febdf92c001b765251e05008ca) supports the feasibility of generating verified F. graminearum deletions, using CRISPR/Cas9 RNP-mediated short-homology integration for PKS12 and confirming deletion by Southern blotting, but this is less direct support for the proposed protoplast transformation and homologous recombination workflow because it uses a different deletion platform and does not report transformation efficiencies, selection markers, or phenotypic measurements. Viewed iteratively, Li 2019 established deletion/complementation phenotyping in F. graminearum, Wang 2023 extended deletion/complementation to virulence and host assays, Lee 2022 introduced a CRISPR/RNP deletion platform, and Wang 2024 integrated deletion with chemical and pathway readouts.",
  "contradictions": "No related work directly contradicts the feasibility of generating F. graminearum deletion mutants, but the strongest methodological contradiction is that the proposed plan relies on protoplast transformation, homologous recombination, selection markers, and PCR-only verification, whereas newer work by [(Lee et al., 2022-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1a680a3d1713d5febdf92c001b765251e05008ca) demonstrates a CRISPR/Cas9 RNP-mediated short-homology deletion strategy in F. graminearum for PKS12, with in vitro RNP cleavage testing, nuclear localization analysis, and Southern blot confirmation; this suggests the proposed method may be less efficient or less state-of-the-art for difficult targets and that PCR alone may be insufficient to confirm clean homozygous deletions. A second challenge comes from [(Wang et al., 2024-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c825b35e476e9f1a2d424115887c5cfd63259fde), which tied deletion-mutant analysis to specialized readouts such as toxisome-shaped structure formation, DON biosynthesis, and HOG/UPR pathway involvement, and used the chemical ZJU212 to block toxisome formation and DON production; this contradicts the assumption that generic media growth and metabolite supplementation alone will capture quinofumelin's target phenotype, especially if quinofumelin affects subcellular structures, secondary metabolism, or pathogenicity. The weakest but important gap is that the cited works, including [(Li et al., 2019-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/74f9c1b579ff2eac7825af94b8513e54767128cd), [(Wang et al., 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/69e2e73cb03a1faa61b26d3c046a41bdcf6f7429), [(Lee et al., 2022-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1a680a3d1713d5febdf92c001b765251e05008ca), and [(Wang et al., 2024-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c825b35e476e9f1a2d424115887c5cfd63259fde), lack quantitative deletion frequencies, statistical significance, and metabolite-rescue experiments, so the proposed use of mutant phenotypes with and without metabolite supplementation to infer gene essentiality and pathway involvement is not directly supported by the cited evidence and must be treated as an untested inference rather than an established outcome.",
  "suggested_action": "The most important improvement is to add a CRISPR/Cas9 RNP short-homology deletion option alongside protoplast transformation and homologous recombination, as demonstrated by [(Lee et al., 2022-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1a680a3d1713d5febdf92c001b765251e05008ca) for PKS12 in F. graminearum, including sgRNA design, in vitro RNP cleavage validation, nuclear localization checks if needed, and confirmation by Southern blot or long-range PCR rather than PCR alone. The next priority is to define a quantitative phenotyping panel that matches the research plan's quinofumelin treatment of F. graminearum PH-1 at 1 μg/mL and the related work's readouts: colony diameter and growth rate on CZA and other media, conidial germination, sexual reproduction/fertility, pathogenicity on wheat or susceptible hosts, DON production, and toxisome microscopy if relevant, with biological replicates, ANOVA, Fisher's PLSD, and pre-specified fold-change or significance thresholds. The third priority is to formalize the metabolite-supplementation rescue design, because none of the cited studies, including [(Li et al., 2019-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/74f9c1b579ff2eac7825af94b8513e54767128cd), [(Wang et al., 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/69e2e73cb03a1faa61b26d3c046a41bdcf6f7429), or [(Wang et al., 2024-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c825b35e476e9f1a2d424115887c5cfd63259fde), provides direct evidence that exogenous metabolites rescue quinofumelin-induced inhibition; the plan should specify metabolite concentrations, time courses, dose-response curves, rescue criteria, essentiality controls, complementation strains, and quinofumelin sensitivity/resistance assays. Finally, include complementation and, where possible, conditional expression or overexpression for essential candidate genes, as supported by [(Li et al., 2019-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/74f9c1b579ff2eac7825af94b8513e54767128cd) and [(Wang et al., 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/69e2e73cb03a1faa61b26d3c046a41bdcf6f7429), to distinguish target-specific pathway effects from general growth defects.",
  "soundness_score": 7
}
